.TH INTRO 4
.SH NAME
intro \- servers and devices: the ports programs talk to
.SH DESCRIPTION
The kernel offers very little: processes and threads, memory, time,
message passing and, for drivers, access to devices.
Everything else (the terminal, the disk, files, users, the network) is done
by ordinary programs, the
.IR servers ,
that other programs talk to with messages.
This section describes each server: its port, how it starts, and the
messages it understands.
.PP
A server creates a named
.B port
.RB ( port_create (2))
and waits on it.
A client finds the port by name
.RB ( port_lookup (2))
and sends a message
.RB ( ipc_call (2)):
w[0] is the operation, w[1]..w[3] small values, and larger data travels in
sbuf (to the server) or rbuf (back to the client).
The reply's w[0] is the result: >= 0, or a negative error number.
The kernel stamps each message with the sender's pid, uid and gid, which a
server can trust to decide what the caller may do.
The message formats are in
.BR <mk/proto.h> .
.PP
.B Restarts.
A server that crashes is started again by the supervisor,
.BR init (8),
about 1 ms later.
The library's
.B call_named
finds the port again by name and sends the call again when the old one
failed with \-ENOENT (a stale port) or, for requests safe to repeat, \-EPIPE
(the server died during the call); open files are reopened.
.PP
.B On demand.
Some servers are not started at boot: init declares their port to the
kernel, the first program that looks it up waits, and init starts the server.
After its idle time without use, init asks it
.B SVC_MAYSTOP
("may you stop?"); a server that holds no client state says yes and ends.
Every server answers an operation it does not know with \-ENOSYS at once,
which is how init's
.B SVC_PING
("are you there?") works: a core server that does not answer for 30 s is
stuck, and is ended and started again.
.SH SERVERS
.nf
port       program  started               page
console    con      boot module, driver   con(4)
usb        usb      boot module, driver   usb(4)
disk       vblk     boot module, driver   vblk(4)
fs         fs       boot module           fs(4)
init       init     the kernel            init(8)
auth       auth     on demand (5 min)     auth(8)
acpi       acpid    at boot, x86-64       acpid(4)
atlas      atlas    at boot, driver       atlas(4)
nic0       vnet     on demand (10 min)    vnet(4)
net        netd     on demand (10 min)    netd(4)
wifi       wifid    at boot, driver       wifid(4)
bt         btd      at boot, driver       btd(4)
audio      audiod   on demand, kept       audiod(4)
gpio       gpiod    on demand, kept       gpiod(4)
unix       unixd    on demand (10 min)    unixd(4)
sia        siad     on demand (15 min)    siad(4)
\-          i2chid   at boot, x86-64       i2chid(4)
\-          ipts     at boot, x86-64       ipts(4)
.fi
.PP
A server with no hardware to drive (no ACPI tables, no Wi-Fi chip, no
display card) ends with status 0 at start, and init does not start it again.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /bin
the servers that are not boot modules
